FileOutputStream
Earlier, you learned how to
write text to files
using
FileWriter
.
The
FileOutputStream
class works in a similar way, but it writes data as
raw bytes
.  
That means you can use it not only for text files, but also for
binary files
(like images, PDFs, or audio).
Write a Text File (Basic Example)
This example writes a short text string to a file using
FileOutputStream
.
Note:
If the file already exists, its contents will be replaced (overwritten).
Example
import java.io.FileOutputStream;
import java.io.IOException;

public class Main {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    // The text we want to write
    String text = "Hello World!";

    // try-with-resources: stream will be closed automatically
    try (FileOutputStream output = new FileOutputStream("filename.txt")) {
      output.write(text.getBytes());  // convert text to bytes and write
      System.out.println("Successfully wrote to file.");
    } catch (IOException e) {
      System.out.println("Error writing file.");
      e.printStackTrace();
    }
  }
}
The output will be:
Successfully wrote to file.
Run Example »
Explanation:
This program creates (or overwrites)
filename.txt
and writes
Hello World!
into it. If everything works, it prints
"Successfully wrote to file."
in the console. Otherwise, it shows an error message.
